Poling: SMT Aided Linearizability Proofs -- building and running
=================================================================

This file describes how to build Poling on a current system.  It
replaces the "Installation" section of README, which describes the
original 2015 setup (Yices 1 through a SWIG binding, OCaml 3.12/4.0x)
that no longer builds.  The usage and annotation documentation in
README is still accurate.

Tested on Ubuntu 24.04 (x86-64) with OCaml 5.2.1 and OCaml 4.14.4, GCC
13 and Yices 2.7.0.


===[ What changed with respect to the original distribution ]=========

* SMT solver.  Poling was linked against Yices 1.0.38 through a SWIG
  binding (external/yices, which only contains macOS binaries).  Yices 1
  can no longer be downloaded from SRI.  Poling now uses Yices 2, the
  open-source successor (GPLv3, https://github.com/SRI-CSL/yices2),
  through a small hand-written binding:

    oyices.mli, oyices.ml, oyices_stubs.c

  This binding keeps the names and calling conventions of the subset
  of the Yices 1 API that theoremProverYices.ml uses, so the prover
  interface is otherwise unchanged.  The directory external/yices is no
  longer used.

* theoremProverYices.ml.  Yices 2 type-checks every term; Yices 1 (whose
  type checker is off by default) silently accepted ill-sorted terms.
  Two encodings were adjusted accordingly:
    - recursive predicates such as "sorted" are now uninterpreted
      int -> bool functions (they were int -> int functions used as
      formulas);
    - declarations are memoized on (name, type) instead of name only,
      because the same name can occur at several types, e.g. the program
      variable "next" and the field function "next".
  The -log_queries option is accepted but ignored (Yices 2 has no API
  log file).

* OCaml.  The sources were ported to current OCaml: Pervasives is now
  Stdlib, and the removed operator (&) is now (&&).  This requires OCaml
  4.08 or later.

* Makefile.  "make yices" (alias "make libs") builds Yices 2; the
  native and bytecode targets link the binding statically against it.
  The optional LablTk GUI target (poling-gui, gui.ml) was not updated.


===[ Prerequisites ]=================================================

* OCaml >= 4.08 with ocamlopt, ocamllex and ocamlyacc (the str and unix
  libraries ship with the compiler; no opam packages are needed)
* GNU make, a C compiler, and a C preprocessor (cpp)
* GMP, including its headers
* To build Yices 2 from source: curl, autoconf and gperf

On Debian/Ubuntu:

  sudo apt install build-essential libgmp-dev autoconf gperf curl

OCaml can come from opam (for example "opam switch create 5.2.1") or
from the distribution ("sudo apt install ocaml").


===[ Building ]======================================================

From the top-level directory:

  make yices    # download, build and install Yices 2 into external/yices2
  make          # build the native executable ./poling

"make yices" downloads the Yices 2.7.0 sources from GitHub, builds them
in a temporary directory (about a minute) without MCSAT (so libpoly and
CUDD are not needed), and copies only the headers and the static
library libyices.a into external/yices2.  Since Yices is linked
statically, ./poling only needs libgmp at run time.  You only need to
run it once; "make clean" does not remove it.

To use a Yices 2 that is already installed, skip "make yices" and point
YICES_DIR at its prefix (the directory with include/yices.h and
lib/libyices.*):

  make YICES_DIR=/usr/local

If the library found there is a shared one, it must be on the run-time
linker path (e.g. via LD_LIBRARY_PATH).

Other targets:

  make poling.byte   # bytecode executable (built with -custom)
  make clean         # remove build products (keeps external/yices2)

"make clean" also deletes lexer.ml and parser.ml, which are generated
from lexer.mll and parser.mly (and regenerated by the next build) but
are checked into the repository.

Do not use the prebuilt Yices 2 release tarballs from GitHub with
"make": their libyices.a needs libpoly and CUDD, and their libyices.so
needs libcudd at run time.

Note: Yices' own build system does not support directory names
containing spaces, which is why external/build-yices2.sh builds it in a
temporary directory.  Poling itself builds fine in such directories.


===[ Checking the build ]=============================================

  ./poling -help
  cpp EXAMPLES/Treiber_stack.cav | ./poling -mcpa_linear

The second command should end with "Valid".


===[ Running the benchmarks ]=========================================

As in README, each benchmark is piped through cpp.  Use -mcpa_linear
for the Poling algorithm and -linear for the CAVE algorithm.
Benchmarks that need extra flags:

  cpp EXAMPLES/Lockcouping_set.cav | ./poling -mcpa_linear -no_leaks
  cpp EXAMPLES/HSY_stack.cav       | ./poling -mcpa_linear -ifile EXAMPLES/HSY_stack.inv
  cpp EXAMPLES/HW_queue.cav        | ./poling -mcpa_linear -ifile EXAMPLES/HW_queue.inv

(the buggy variants in EXAMPLES/buggy take the same flags, using
EXAMPLES/buggy/HSY_stack.inv).  The *_spec.cav files are included by
the other examples and are not run on their own.

Results with this build (Ubuntu 24.04, OCaml 5.2.1, Yices 2.7.0,
-mcpa_linear; times as reported by Poling, with 8 benchmarks running in
parallel), next to the Poling times from Fig. 8 of the paper:

  EXAMPLES/              result               time     paper
  ---------------------  -----------------  -------  -------
  Lockcouping_set.cav    Valid                1.73s    4.01s
  Vechev_DCAS_set.cav    Valid                1.44s    3.15s
  2lock_queue.cav        Valid                1.02s    2.51s
  Treiber_stack.cav      Valid                0.02s    0.06s
  MSqueue.cav            Valid                0.63s    1.12s
  DGLMqueue.cav          Valid                1.46s    1.47s
  Vechev_CAS_set.cav     Valid              349.33s  868.44s
  Optimistic_set.cav     Valid               10.85s   27.51s
  Lazy_set.cav           Valid              141.54s  321.78s
  ccas.cav               Valid                0.23s    0.82s
  HSY_stack.cav          assertion failure        -    5.98s  (see below)
  HW_queue.cav           Valid                0.02s        -

All programs in EXAMPLES/buggy are reported "NOT Valid", except
HSY_stack_bug2.cav and HSY_stack_bug3.cav, which hit the same assertion
failure as HSY_stack.cav.  (EXAMPLES/Array.cav has no linearizability
specification and is not one of the benchmarks.)

As a cross-check, the unmodified sources were also built with OCaml
4.02.3 against the original Yices 1.0.40 binding: all 24 programs above
(benchmarks and buggy variants) give the same verdicts as this build.

Known issue: HSY_stack.cav (and buggy/HSY_stack_bug2.cav,
buggy/HSY_stack_bug3.cav) stop with

  Warning: linearizability cannot be ensured!
  Fatal error: exception File "symbsimp.ml", line 2471, ...: Assertion failed

while checking the pure specification of pop.  The paper reports that
HSY stack is proved in 5.98s.  This failure is not caused by the port:
the unmodified sources of this repository, built with OCaml 4.02.3
against the original SWIG binding and Yices 1.0.40 (x86-64 Linux, the
last Yices 1 release), fail in exactly the same way.  The failure is
also independent of the OCaml version (4.02.3, 4.14.4, 5.2.1) and of the
preprocessor (GNU cpp or clang).  This snapshot of the artifact
apparently differs from the version evaluated in the paper.
